import
	../../html

intro-interfaces html[]()
	- section "Interfaces",
		- p quote
			An {interface_} type is like a union, but does not declare its case types.
			{br}
			Instead, each case type declares itself a {case_} of the interface.

		- keen-runnable quote
			import
				keen/math/math

			main void()
				a shape = circle 1
				b shape = square 2
				log area a
				log area b

			shape interface
				area float()

			circle record(radius float) shape case
			area float(a circle)
				pi * a.radius ** 2

			square record(width float) shape case
			area float(a square)
				a.width ** 2

	- section "Strong interfaces",
		- p quote
			There's no way to retrieve the case type given a reference to an interface.
			{br}
			The only thing you can do with an {interface_} value is call its methods.
			{br}
			This is usually a good thing as it makes an interface a meaningful specification
			of what a value is actually used for.
			{br}
			If you want to {match_} on an interface, use a {variant_} instead.

	- section "Interface mutability",
		- p quote
			As with a {union_}, {case_} types of an {interface_} can have at most the interface's mutability.

		- keen-runnable quote
			main void()
				a i = r 0
				log update a
				log update a

			i interface mut
				update nat()
			r record mut, i case
				count mut nat
			update nat(a r)
				a.count +:= 1
				a.count

		- aside-small "Try removing {mut_} from the interface above to see the error."

	- section "Variants",
		- p quote
			{variant_}s are like interfaces, but support {match_} expressions.
			{br}
			Or to put it another way, a {variant_} is like a {union_}
			where the case types do not have to be known up-front.
			{br}
			These aren't often needed, but the {exception} type is an example variant type.
			It's a good example where it would be impractical to declare all case types up-front.

		- keen-runnable quote
			main void()
				a shape = circle 1
				log match a
				as circle
					"circle \{a.radius\}"
				as square
					"square \{a.width\}"
				else
					"some other type"

			shape variant
			circle record(radius float) shape case
			square record(width float) shape case

		- aside "Variants are open",
			- p quote
				Since any type can declare itself a {case_} of the variant, variants must be treated as open-ended.
				{br}
				No code can be assumed to know all case types.
			- p "So, the {else_} in the above example is mandatory, even though no other case types are in sight."
			- p "If you want a closed set of case types, use a {union_}."

	- section "Restrictions",
		- bullets
			- quote
				Since a case type must explicitly {case_} an interface or variant,
				they can't contain already-declared types like {nat}.
			- "Auto functions won't work on interfaces or variants."
